VALUES (460091351101011, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Engineering curves and their practical classification. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Constructing an ellipse by standard geometric methods. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Parabola and hyperbola construction and the focus/directrix idea. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Cycloidal curves, involutes and spirals. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nEngineering curves include conics and other geometric curves used to represent shapes or motion. Ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas are conic sections; cycloids, epicycloids, hypocycloids, involutes and spirals are other common constructions in engineering graphics. The method is chosen according to the defining geometry and the intended use. A drawing should show construction lines clearly, use suitable instruments and identify the required tangent or normal when requested. Accuracy depends on the construction procedure and neat, unambiguous line work.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn ellipse can be constructed by methods such as concentric circles, rectangle or oblong methods, and arc-of-circle approaches taught in the syllabus. First mark the major and minor axes and their centres accurately. Divide the relevant circles, sides or arcs into corresponding parts, project matching points and draw a smooth continuous curve through them. Select the method according to the data and construction requested. Check symmetry about both axes and use light construction lines before darkening the final curve.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA conic is defined by a geometric relation between a point and a focus/directrix or by another specified construction condition. A parabola has eccentricity one; an ellipse has eccentricity below one and a hyperbola above one. Construction methods use the given dimensions or geometric properties to locate successive points, then join them smoothly. Tangents and normals are drawn according to the prescribed method at the required point. Verify the defining dimensions and symmetry and distinguish the conics by their geometry rather than by appearance alone.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA cycloid is traced by a point on a circle rolling along a straight line. An epicycloid is generated when a circle rolls outside another circle, while a hypocycloid is generated when it rolls inside. An involute can be understood as the path traced by the end of a taut string unwound from a base curve; spirals move progressively inward or outward according to their construction. Start from the generating geometry, divide the motion into corresponding steps, plot points carefully and construct any requested tangent or normal.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

-- ENGINEERING DRAWING | Mid 1 | easy | 460091351101012 | theory
INSERT INTO public."TestsData" ("TestId", "Questions", "Options", "Answers")
VALUES (460091351101012, ARRAY['"[8 marks] Explain Engineering curves and their practical classification. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Constructing an ellipse by standard geometric methods. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Projection of points in the principal planes and quadrants. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb, '"[8 marks] Explain Projection of lines and finding true length and inclination. Describe its meaning or purpose, principal features or steps, and one relevant example."'::jsonb]::jsonb[], ARRAY[]::jsonb[], ARRAY['"Model answer:\nEngineering curves include conics and other geometric curves used to represent shapes or motion. Ellipses, parabolas and hyperbolas are conic sections; cycloids, epicycloids, hypocycloids, involutes and spirals are other common constructions in engineering graphics. The method is chosen according to the defining geometry and the intended use. A drawing should show construction lines clearly, use suitable instruments and identify the required tangent or normal when requested. Accuracy depends on the construction procedure and neat, unambiguous line work.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nAn ellipse can be constructed by methods such as concentric circles, rectangle or oblong methods, and arc-of-circle approaches taught in the syllabus. First mark the major and minor axes and their centres accurately. Divide the relevant circles, sides or arcs into corresponding parts, project matching points and draw a smooth continuous curve through them. Select the method according to the data and construction requested. Check symmetry about both axes and use light construction lines before darkening the final curve.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nOrthographic projection represents a 3D point by projections on principal reference planes, commonly the horizontal plane (HP) and vertical plane (VP). A point''s position relative to HP and VP determines its quadrant and the location of its front and top views. Use the reference line consistently, draw projectors perpendicular to it and apply the convention for distances from each plane. Label views clearly and preserve the given dimensions. Errors often result from swapping the meanings of distance above/below HP and in front of/behind VP.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb, '"Model answer:\nA line parallel to a reference plane appears in true length on that plane''s projection; when inclined, its projection is generally foreshortened. For a line inclined to one or both planes, use the prescribed change-of-position or component method, keeping endpoints and projectors consistent. True length and true inclinations are recovered from a view in which the line''s full length is represented or from the appropriate construction. Show intermediate positions and label views. Check that apparent projected length does not exceed true length and that given endpoint coordinates remain consistent.\n\n8-mark marking guide: 2 marks for the core concept; 3 marks for accurate principles, steps or formulas; 2 marks for an example/application; 1 mark for a justified conclusion or check. A strong answer should define the concept and organize the main points clearly."'::jsonb]::jsonb[]);

